  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which 3-Epi-20-Hydroxyecdysone may interact with LNCaP cells, through a series of molecular targets that ultimately lead to cellular effects on the cancerous prostate line. The drug is implicated in targeting Thioredoxin, a protein involved in cellular redox regulation and apoptosis, which is also a target of several other compounds, including Phorone and a synthetic derivative. These targets are all associated with LNCaP, a prostate cancer cell line, indicating a possible pathway where 3-Epi-20-Hydroxyecdysone may modulate cellular processes in the tumor. The evidence is supported by a consistent pattern of interaction, with the drug acting as a central node in a network of targets that ultimately influence LNCaP cells. The mechanism is supported by the presence of a final target in the network, suggesting a plausible biological pathway that could be relevant to the drug's therapeutic potential.",
